---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { getDeals, getPrincipals } from '../lib/deals';

const deals = getDeals();
const analyzed = deals.filter((d) => d.analysis && d.analysis.verdict !== 'pending');
const circular = deals.filter((d) => d.analysis?.verdict === 'circular').length;
const mixed    = deals.filter((d) => d.analysis?.verdict === 'mixed').length;
const buildout = deals.filter((d) => d.analysis?.verdict === 'buildout').length;
---
<Base title="About" description="The argument, the method, the data, the acknowledgements.">
  <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
  <h1 style="max-width: 22ch">About this project</h1>

  <section class="prose">
    <h2 class="smallcaps">The argument</h2>
    <p>
      The current wave of AI mega-deals — OpenAI–Nvidia–Oracle,
      Microsoft–OpenAI, Anthropic–Amazon, AMD–OpenAI, the Stargate
      consortium — does not look like a normal industrial buildout. The
      same dollars appear on both sides of the ledger: a chip company
      invests in a model company that commits to buying compute from a
      cloud company that buys chips from the chip company. The same
      check is recognized as an investment gain on one income statement
      and as cloud revenue on another.
    </p>
    <p>
      The argument here is that the industry is structurally a small
      number of people moving very large sums in patterns that recur
      across deals. The same dozen figures — Altman, Amodei, Huang,
      Nadella, Hassabis, Ellison, Su, Musk, Son, Jassy, Pichai,
      Zuckerberg, Intrator — appear repeatedly as counterparties,
      often in arrangements where the line between customer, investor,
      and supplier is unclear.
    </p>
    <p>
      Following Jill Lepore's observation that AI governance has
      collapsed into the personalities of a few private actors, this
      site argues the same about AI capital flows. The dollars do not
      come from a normal market with thousands of independent
      counterparties; they come from a court.
    </p>

    <h2 class="smallcaps">The method</h2>
    <p>
      The catalog draws its underlying deal data from
      <a href="https://github.com/jstwng/compute-deal-map-data">jstwng/compute-deal-map-data</a>,
      a community-maintained, source-backed corpus of {deals.length}
      AI-ecosystem transactions licensed CC&nbsp;BY&nbsp;4.0. Every deal
      carries a primary-source URL.
    </p>
    <p>
      On top of that substrate, this project adds three contributions:
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>1. The principals layer.</strong> The upstream data is
      company-level. The argument is about <em>people</em>. So a
      <code>principals.yml</code> file maps each company to its
      public-face decision-maker(s), letting the site show — by simple
      join — how often each figure appears across deals.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>2. The analysis overlay.</strong> Each analyzed deal gets
      a structured reading: a <em>verdict</em> (buildout, circular,
      mixed, pending), a <em>sustainability rubric</em> scoring the
      deal 0–3 on five criteria, an <em>incentive map</em> describing
      who benefits and what flows where, and <em>circularity notes</em>
      explaining the mechanism by which dollars loop. As of this writing
      {analyzed.length} of {deals.length} deals are analyzed
      ({circular} circular, {mixed} mixed, {buildout} buildout). The
      rest are catalogued without verdict.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>3. The Pantheon.</strong> The Greco-Roman framing is half
      joke and half argument. A market in which twelve figures recur as
      gods of overlapping domains resembles a mythological court more
      than a competitive market. Each principal gets a mythological
      analog whose function does analytical work: Hephaestus (Huang)
      forges what every other god needs; Hermes (Altman) is the
      messenger who makes the deals; Cronus (Ellison) is the old titan
      tolerated by his successors. Deals flagged as circular receive
      satirical illustration treatment; deals flagged as genuine
      buildout receive flattering treatment. The dual register is part
      of the argument — humor makes the incestuous deal network legible
      in a way a spreadsheet cannot.
    </p>

    <h2 class="smallcaps">The rubric, in detail</h2>
    <p>Each analyzed deal is scored 0–3 on five criteria. Higher is more sustainable, lower is more circular.</p>

    <div class="rubric-explainer">
      <div>
        <h3>Real economic demand</h3>
        <p>Does the buyer have non-investor revenue justifying the purchase? Is the headline figure proportional to the buyer's actual workload, or to its press strategy?</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Arms-length pricing</h3>
        <p>Is the price set by competition with independent bidders, or by negotiating-with-yourself between parties that share equity?</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Cash vs. equity clarity</h3>
        <p>Is consideration flowing as cash, equity, in-kind credits, or a murky hybrid (e.g., "$10B investment" denominated largely as compute credits redeemable only with the investor)?</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Counterparty independence</h3>
        <p>Are the buyer and seller financially distinct firms, or do they share major shareholders, board members, or cross-investment such that the "transaction" is largely intra-court bookkeeping?</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Third-party verification</h3>
        <p>Are the terms disclosed in audited filings, regulatory dockets, or only in press releases? Press-release-grade disclosure is treated as weak evidence.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2 class="smallcaps">What "verdict" means</h2>
    <p>
      <span class="badge badge--buildout">Buildout</span> &nbsp;
      Genuine industrial purchase. Arms-length parties, real demand,
      clean instrument, disclosed terms. The control cases in the
      dataset (Apple–TSMC, ASML–TSMC, regulated power PPAs) live here.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="badge badge--mixed">Mixed</span> &nbsp;
      Real demand and substantive engineering, but the financing chain
      includes circular elements. Stargate sits here: the Abilene
      campus is physically being built, but the dollars routing
      through Crusoe → Oracle → OpenAI → NVIDIA form a closed loop
      that the individual contracts disguise.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="badge badge--circular">Circular</span> &nbsp;
      The same dollars appear on both sides of the ledger.
      Investments denominated as captive compute credit;
      "purchases" paired with equity grants of equivalent magnitude;
      cloud contracts where the supplier issues stock to the
      customer. Mechanism varies; the loop closes.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="badge badge--pending">Pending verdict</span> &nbsp;
      Catalogued from the upstream dataset but not yet analyzed. Most
      deals are here. The analytical layer is being filled in deal by
      deal.
    </p>

    <h2 class="smallcaps">What this site is not</h2>
    <p>
      It is not a prediction about whether any specific deal will
      "work." Several deals flagged here as circular will produce real
      products, real revenue, and real returns; the question is not
      whether anyone wins, but whether the dollar figures publicly
      reported as <em>investment</em>, <em>cloud revenue</em>, and
      <em>chip orders</em> can all be true at once. They generally
      cannot.
    </p>
    <p>
      It is not neutral. The framing is editorial. The verdicts are
      arguments. Every deal carries a primary-source URL so readers
      can check the underlying evidence against the reading.
    </p>

    <h2 class="smallcaps">Acknowledgements</h2>
    <p>
      Deal data: <a href="https://github.com/jstwng/compute-deal-map-data">jstwng/compute-deal-map-data</a> (CC&nbsp;BY&nbsp;4.0). Source URLs preserved for every entry.
    </p>
    <p>
      Conceptual scaffolding: Jill Lepore's reporting on AI
      governance, in particular the argument that the regulatory and
      policy conversation has collapsed into the personalities of
      Altman, Hassabis, and Amodei.
    </p>
    <p>
      Built with Astro. Source at <a href="https://github.com/vivekyarla/busgen-project">github.com/vivekyarla/busgen-project</a>. Site code MIT-licensed; overlay text CC&nbsp;BY&nbsp;4.0.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .rubric-explainer {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(280px, 1fr));
    gap: 1.4rem;
    margin: 1.2rem 0 2.5rem;
  }
  .rubric-explainer > div {
    border-left: 2px solid var(--gilt);
    padding: 0.2rem 0 0.4rem 1rem;
  }
  .rubric-explainer h3 {
    font-family: var(--serif);
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    margin: 0 0 0.3rem;
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  .rubric-explainer p {
    font-size: 0.92rem;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    margin: 0;
  }
</style>
